---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy";
const title = "Sequenzy vs Privy for Shopify: 2026 Decision Guide";
const desc = "Keep signup promises intact. A scoped Shopify workflow comparison with current product evidence and proposed acceptance checks.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only a popup tool?",
    "answer": "No. Its current Flows product includes conditional journeys and email/SMS messaging. Evaluate the required follow-up rather than assume a basic automation ceiling."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Sequenzy require Privy for signup capture?",
    "answer": "No. Sequenzy advertises forms and popups. Compare the targeting and offer experience required by your store."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep Privy displays with Sequenzy email?",
    "answer": "Treat this as a proposed split architecture. Verify a supported bridge and trace source, consent, suppression and repeat-signup behavior before enabling it."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens to the coupon when I switch senders?",
    "answer": "Assign one coupon-delivery owner and preserve the offer identifier. Test repeat signup and purchaser exclusion before replacing the flow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which cost units should I compare?",
    "answer": "Compare Privy mailable-contact email or displays-only pageviews with Sequenzy email sends, plus any bridge and SMS cost. Use the intended configuration."
  },
  {
    "question": "What proves the signup handoff works?",
    "answer": "A controlled signup should retain its promised offer, permission and source in the destination and enter exactly the intended journey. A customer sync alone is insufficient."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14 md:py-20">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Shopify decision guide</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Sequenzy vs Privy</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">For a merchant choosing popups and a follow-up sender together, the useful comparison is the complete signup experience: what the shopper was promised, which consent was collected, and which flow delivers it. Privy is also an email/SMS automation platform; Sequenzy also advertises forms and popups. Buying both requires a specific reason.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current overlap matters</h2>
      <p>Privy launched visual Flows in July 2025 with triggers, delays, conditions, email/SMS steps and conditional splits. Its May 2026 updates include signup tags and flows triggered by Shopify or Privy tag changes. Sequenzy documents signup capture alongside Shopify email flows. The old claim that Privy can only send a basic drip, or that Sequenzy necessarily requires a separate capture app, is an unsuitable basis for buying.</p>
      <h2>Proposed trial: the promised coupon</h2>
      <p>For a hypothetical board-game store, create a popup offering a first-order coupon and asking for preferred game type. Submit an email-only signup. Inspect where the offer identifier, source and preference arrive, which welcome flow starts, and whether the promised code appears correctly. Next submit the same address again. The acceptance criterion is a deliberate repeat-signup policy, not two accidental welcome sequences. This is a proposed test, with no measured conversion result.</p>
      <h2>Keep email and SMS permission distinct</h2>
      <p>Add a second test visitor who accepts email but declines SMS. Inspect the stored permission and ensure no SMS flow enrolls the visitor merely because a phone number exists. Then use a visitor with the opposite channel choice if the form supports it. Preserve the actual form version and offer wording with the record. Destination and legal requirements need their own review; a form design does not by itself prove permission for every campaign.</p>
      <h2>When one platform is enough</h2>
      <p>Start by implementing the needed capture and welcome journey within each candidate. Ask the merchant to change the offer, target a page group and exclude an existing purchaser. If the chosen tool handles these jobs and the subsequent purchase follow-up, a second app adds subscription cost and another synchronization boundary. There is no order-count threshold at which Privy automatically stops being useful for retention.</p>
      <h2>When keeping Privy capture is justified</h2>
      <p>An existing Privy display may have targeting, creative and operational ownership the team wants to retain while evaluating Sequenzy email. In that case, verify a supported transfer rather than assume a direct Privy-to-Sequenzy integration. Trace a real test signup through the selected bridge, preserving source, consent and unsubscribe state. A Shopify customer record or tag should not be treated as proof that all those fields reach the destination.</p>
      <h2>A tag is not the whole event</h2>
      <p>Privy documents tag-triggered Flows, which can be useful for moving a contact into a journey. Specify who adds and removes a tag and whether repeated additions can restart the welcome. If a source tag changes later, decide whether that should update preference or issue another coupon. For a split setup, capture transport errors and assign an owner to reconcile missing signups rather than silently losing the offer promise.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate economics without a fake lift</h2>
      <p>Privy separates mailable-contact email pricing from pageview-based displays-only pricing. Sequenzy advertises email-send billing with unlimited contacts. Request a quote for the actual configuration, including capture-only if relevant. Compare one platform with a split stack using the same audience and expected sends. Add bridge maintenance and coupon support work. Do not fund the extra app on an invented repeat-purchase improvement.</p>
      <h2>Choose from the complete signup path</h2>
      <p>Prefer a single platform when its display targeting, welcome logic and purchase follow-up meet the brief. Keep Privy capture beside Sequenzy only when the retained display value and verified handoff justify operating both. Disable duplicate welcome and coupon delivery paths before cutover. Recheck existing-customer targeting after changing the theme or display rules; a new popup can undermine an otherwise correct lifecycle flow.</p>
      <h2>Sources and evaluation limits</h2>
      <p>Supplier documentation checked November 24, 2026. These are documented capabilities and proposed decision checks, not firsthand product tests or measured merchant results. Account eligibility, payloads and current quotes need confirmation before activation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/changelog/2025-07-02-new-flows">Privy Flows launch</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/changelog/judge-me-smarter-product-blocks-better-flow-control">Privy tag and flow updates</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy billing units</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/about">Sequenzy capture scope</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo">Sequenzy vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-omnisend">Sequenzy vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email">Sequenzy vs Shopify Email</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email apps for jewelry stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">Welcome series playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs Privy FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
